,"height":1275,"pagecount":13,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/FIRST_AID_FOR_POISIONING_AND_OVERDOSES_%28IA_NAVMEDP5095%29.pdf/page1-960px-FIRST_AID_FOR_POISIONING_AND_OVERDOSES_%28IA_NAVMEDP5095%29.pdf.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":640,"thumbheight":495,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/FIRST_AID_FOR_POISIONING_AND_OVERDOSES_%28IA_NAVMEDP5095%29.pdf/page1-1280px-FIRST_AID_FOR_POISIONING_AND_OVERDOSES_%28IA_NAVMEDP5095%29.pdf.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/FIRST_AID_FOR_POISIONING_AND_OVERDOSES_%28IA_NAVMEDP5095%29.pdf/page1-960px-FIRST_AID_FOR_POISIONING_AND_OVERDOSES_%28IA_NAVMEDP5095%29.pdf.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":640,"height":495,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/FIRST_AID_FOR_POISIONING_AND_OVERDOSES_%28IA_NAVMEDP5095%29.pdf/page1-1280px-FIRST_AID_FOR_POISIONING_AND_OVERDOSES_%28IA_NAVMEDP5095%29.pdf.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0f/FIRST_AID_FOR_POISIONING_AND_OVERDOSES_%28IA_NAVMEDP5095%29.pdf?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FIRST_AID_FOR_POISIONING_AND_OVERDOSES_(IA_NAVMEDP5095).pdf","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=91645570","extmetadata":{"Artist":{"value":"<div class=\"fn value\">\nU.S. Navy. Bureau of Medicine and Surgery</div>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"ImageDescription":{"value":"<div class=\"description\">\n<p>NAVMED P-5095<br><br>1. This publication is intended to serve as an instruction<br>for non-medical personnel for the first aid treatment for<br>poisonings or exposure to poisons. Medical aid should be<br>sought in all cases as soon as feasible. These guidelines are<br>general and should not be substituted for sound judgment<br>and common sense.<br>2. A toxin (or poison) is a substance which through its<br>chemical action usually kills, injures, or impairs a person.<br>A toxic exposure may not cause an individual to have<br>symptoms immediately. When symptoms develop, the individual<br>is considered poisoned. One of the goals of first<br>aid is to prevent toxic exposures from becoming poisonings.<br>3. An overdose is the excessive administration of a substance<br>that taken in small amounts would not necessarily<br>be considered toxic, but in large amounts can be toxic.<br>4. A poisoning/ overdose treatment locker should be located<br>so that it is readily accessible and visible to all personnel. It<br>should be unlocked but must be sealed. The locker should<br>contain the items set forth in Part II. All contents must be<br>clearly labeled. Additional items may be added to meet<br>local needs. Concise and clear directions for their use must<br>be provided.<br>5. The items contained in the antidote locker (Part II) may<br>be administered by non-medical personnel following the<br>directions given.<br>6. General instructions for the management of toxic exposures<br>are given in Part III. Initial management for exposure<br>to certain types of substances is contained in Part IV.<br>Cross-references for specific substances, including common<br>drugs of abuse and their \"street\" or slang names, are<br>contained in Part V.<br>\n</p>\n<br>Subjects: poison</div>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}},"mime":"application/pdf"}]},"91443302":{"pageid":91443302,"ns":6,"title":"File:HEARING VI ON THE DEPARTMENT OF VETERANS AFFAIRS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY PROGRAMS (IA gov.gpo.fdsys